engine-qualify: cannot persist qualifier evidence: restrictive evidence c4c20d3dc04e3e0f7fb27e9110d75d2d1ae991c3c2426daf9d212f8bd1cb3a7a is not bound to the active qualification
Usage:
  scripts/engine-qualify.sh <reviewer|owner|brain|verification_author|implementer|consult|discuss|foreman>
    --engine <display-id> --model <exact-model-id> --model-version <version>
    --runner <name> --runner-version <version> --family <family>
    --harness-version <version> --effort <effort>
    --prompt-config-hash <sha256> --semantic-fingerprint <sha256>
    --containment-fingerprint <sha256>
    (--panel-cmd '<trusted command>' |
      --remote-provider-cmd '<trusted adapter>' --remote-provider <provider-id>)
    [--panel-bind-ro <absolute-source>=</panel/or/auth/path>] [--panel-env <name>]
    [--provider-env <name>] [--remote-timeout-ms <n>]
    --task-class <class> --domain <domain> --language <language> --tool <tool>
    [--trials <n>] [--store <path>] [--emit-row]
    [--seat-file <json>] [--emit-record] [--allow-effort-mismatch]

  --effort is only a label. Before any provider call the label is compared with the effort the
  provider is told to send (QRP_HTTP_EFFORT / QRP_CLI_EFFORT, counted when passed via --provider-env /
  --panel-env). A mismatch exits 2 with no exam and no spend; --allow-effort-mismatch runs it anyway
  and marks the emitted row effort_check_override=true (effort_check stays mismatch). An effort that
  cannot be observed (custom panel command, no QRP_* variable) is never blocked.

  With --seat-file, identity fields that were not passed as flags are derived
  before any exam write (a flag always wins):
    runner-version        scripts/lib/runner-binary.js (fail closed; no "unknown")
    family                familyOfEngine (scripts/lib/stage-write.js) on the model
                          id: named vendors (minimax/abab, kimi/moonshot, glm/zhipu,
                          grok/composer, gpt/codex/o1/o3/o4, claude/opus/sonnet/haiku,
                          qwen/qwq) before gemini/bison/flash; "unknown" exits 2 and
                          tells the caller to pass --family
    semantic-fingerprint  the implementer constant EXPECTED_IMPL_CORPUS_HASH
    harness-version, containment-fingerprint, task-class, domain, language, tool
                          references/qualification-headless-pins.json
  Source precedence (a field is filled from the first source that has it):
    engine, model, model-version, runner, effort, prompt-config-hash:
      the seat file supplies them; an explicit flag that disagrees with the
      seat file value is not an override, it exits 2.
    runner-version, family, semantic-fingerprint: explicit flag, else derived.
    harness-version, containment-fingerprint, task-class, domain, language,
    tool: explicit flag, else the pins file.
  So: flag, then derived, then pins; the seat file wins only for the six
  fields it carries. seat-preview prints the winner per field under "sources".
  Headless derivation is only pinned for the implementer role. Any other role
  that still needs a pinned field exits 2 and names the first missing scope
  flag (task-class, then domain, language, tool). A pins file that is unreadable,
  malformed, or has an unknown key exits 2 and names that pins file and the
  reason. Seat file missing or schema-invalid stays exit 4.

  scripts/engine-qualify.sh seat-preview --seat-file <f> [--json]
    [the same identity flags the real run accepts]
  Runs the same parse and derivation, prints one JSON document on stdout, and
  exits without sitting an exam and without writing anything. Output is JSON
  by default; --json is accepted and identical.
  Schema: an object with role, corpus_version, argv, and sources.
  argv and sources use these keys: engine, model, model-version, runner,
  runner-version, family, harness-version, effort, prompt-config-hash,
  semantic-fingerprint, containment-fingerprint, task-class, domain, language,
  tool. task-class, domain, language, and tool are arrays.
  Each sources value is one of "flag", "seat", "derived", "pinned".
  seat-preview exit codes: 0 success, 2 derivation or precondition failure,
  4 seat file missing or schema-invalid.
    [--raw-dir <path>]   (dump raw per-case exchanges/ledger into this directory;
      also <role>-usage.jsonl, one row per case: wall_s, token counts, solved)
      Implementer impl-dispatch-ledger.jsonl and impl-exchanges.jsonl add, for a
      non-pass case whose dispatch status is not committed: dispatch_error_class
      (the dispatch JSON error_category, or null when that field is absent — never the
      free-text error), dispatch_error, agent_log_tail (last 40 lines after the
      canary in literal/base64/hex/reversed form, the endpoint base_url and its
      host, and any http(s) URL are masked; secrets redacted; 8 KiB cap)
      and agent_log_path (canary masked; a file path, so URLs are left as-is).
      An integrity_violation row also adds integrity_violation_details, each
      {type, path} only — never the canary value.
    [--price-usd-per-mtok-in <n> --price-usd-per-mtok-out <n> --price-as-of <YYYY-MM-DD>
      --price-source <manual|measured> [--price-ref <text>]]   (all four required together;
      without them cost stays source=unknown and usage.usd_per_solved is null)
    [--version-source runtime|operator-asserted]   (CLI transports observe no
      runtime model id — pass operator-asserted so the row says so)

  scripts/engine-qualify.sh corpus-version [--role <role>]
    Print the current corpus_version for every qualification role, or one role,
    as JSON. Runs no exam and writes no files. Call it when a seat file needs
    the live version, including after the foreman asset pins move.
    corpus-version --help prints this usage text and exits 0.

  implementer is LIVE-RAIL: no panel/remote broker transport — cases dispatch
  through scripts/dispatch-hetero.sh (plan 2026-08-22-implementer-qualification-
  suite). Implementer-only flags:
    [--dispatch-bin <path>]     (default scripts/dispatch-hetero.sh; test seam)
    [--runner-bin <path>]       (forwarded to the rail's per-runner bin seam —
      --grok-bin/--codex-bin/--agy-bin/... — so a smoke can substitute ONLY the
      paid engine while the real rail runs)
    [--dispatch-timeout <dur>]  (per-case rail timeout, default corpus 600s)
    [--endpoint <name>]         (--runner cc-shim ONLY: resolve ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL/AUTH_TOKEN
      through scripts/resolve-endpoint.sh — the SAME named-endpoint definition daily
      routing uses — instead of the raw env passthrough; not-ready exits 2 uncharged;
      the emitted row discloses endpoint {name, transport_security}; base_url stays on the rail only)
  Qualification has no expiry: there is no --expires-days (it is refused). A seat loses standing
  only through accumulated strikes (references/strike-decay.md), and regains it by re-sitting.

  consult and discuss are QUALIFICATION-SEAT roles (never live-rail): they take
  the same --panel-cmd / --remote-provider-cmd broker transport as reviewer/owner
  and REJECT --dispatch-bin/--runner-bin/--dispatch-timeout with a usage error.
  Both support [--plan]: a dry-run that verifies the five frozen identities
  (generator, grader, corpus, rubric, seal), runs the corpus admission gates,
  prints the case plan, and exits WITHOUT any provider/broker call. [--plan]
  combined with an implementer-only flag exits 2 (the flags are rejected before
  --plan is ever consulted).

  A LIVE (non-plan) consult/discuss administration additionally requires
  [--execute] — real money, real provider calls. Without it, the command
  refuses loud, naming this flag and the Board authorization in
  docs/plans/evidence/2026-08-28-consult-discuss-qualify/PROPOSAL.md
  ("Board decision — 2026-08-28 (authorization)"). --execute requires the
  --remote-provider-cmd/--remote-provider (case-broker) transport — the bare
  --panel-cmd transport has no identity binding and is refused for these
  two roles.

The qualifier generates fresh role-specific known-bad, clean, and defect-reversal trials.
Reviewer output uses {"verdict":"pass|fail","findings":[...]} with a structured
behavioral-call-v1 witness. Owner output uses
{"decision":"accept|reject","violations":[...]} against a distinct intent/control corpus.
Local panels run in a fresh fail-closed bubblewrap sandbox. Remote panels cross a one-use Unix
socket to a host broker; credentials and network access never enter the evaluator sandbox.
The repository, corpus, oracle, host home, and previous cases are not mounted or disclosed.
JSONL output is diagnostic telemetry, not admission authority. Only a live in-process
host-observed run can create a session-local role-capability verifier; serializing the run
destroys that capability.

Exit codes:
  0 = qualification passed
  1 = qualification failed
  2 = usage/precondition error
  With --seat-file or --emit-record, 0, 1, and 2 stay for those outcomes and
  these codes also apply. Without either flag, 3, 4, and 5 are not used, and a
  no_verdict run stays exit 1 (the 1-versus-3 split is headless only):
  0 = qualification passed
  1 = real qualification failure (status failed)
  2 = usage or precondition error that is not about the seat file
  3 = no_verdict or an infrastructure failure (the exam could not reach a verdict)
  4 = seat file missing, unreadable, or schema-invalid
  5 = emitted record failed validateRecord or checkRecordSemantics
